This actually reads as two different products that reinforce each other:

The simulation engine (interactive strategy game).
The Thomas Bresche narrative (the campaign/story mode).

The compelling idea isn't the espionage itself—it's that the story teaches systems thinking through consequences.

A structure like this could work well:

Campaign

Thomas starts with almost nothing.

He gradually builds:

knowledge
software
AI agents
businesses
infrastructure
reputation
partnerships

As his influence grows, the type of challenges changes.

Early game:

cash flow
poor architecture
technical debt
bad hiring
scaling problems

Mid game:

competitors
acquisitions
legal disputes
IP protection
insider threats
regulatory compliance

Late game:

multinational operations
geopolitical risk
sanctions
supply-chain disruption
infrastructure resilience
trust and governance

That progression is more believable than immediately introducing intelligence agencies.

Claudia Cechov

As a fictional character, she works best if she's one example of a broader game mechanic rather than the central twist.

She could represent an "Unknown Trust Variable."

The player never knows exactly where the weakness will appear.

It could be:

a business partner
a supplier
an employee
an investor
an AI agent with excessive permissions
a compromised dependency
a romantic relationship
a cloud vendor
an acquisition

The lesson becomes:

Every growing system accumulates trust relationships. Every trust relationship introduces risk.

That scales beyond one plotline.

The Real Mechanic

Behind the scenes, the game could continuously evaluate:

Architecture

Trust

Security

Legal

Financial

Operational

Human

Political

Technical

Every decision affects those dimensions.

A shortcut taken early may not have consequences until many in-game years later.

Endgame

The strongest part of your idea is the post-game analysis.

Instead of "You Win," the game generates a comprehensive report.

For example:

KINGDOM ANALYSIS

Infrastructure Score

Security Score

Leadership Score

Financial Stability

Operational Resilience

Delegation

Automation

Documentation

Technical Debt

Legal Exposure

Trust Network

Business Continuity

Recovery Capability

It then identifies where the player's downfall—or success—came from.

Examples:

Single point of failure in authentication.
Excessive dependence on one supplier.
Poor documentation.
Over-centralised decision making.
Inadequate succession planning.
Weak financial reserves.
Excessive permissions granted to AI agents.
Wizard 2.0

The next playthrough isn't a reset—it starts from lessons learned.

The game could generate:

a revised organisational blueprint
an improved infrastructure architecture
recommended governance policies
a different AI delegation strategy
revised trust and permission models

So each campaign produces a better starting architecture for the next.

Keep the Fiction Clearly Fictional

Elements such as Claudia, intelligence agencies, and international espionage work well as fictional narrative devices if they're clearly presented as part of the Thomas Bresche story. For the simulation itself, it's stronger to model broad concepts like trust, insider risk, business continuity, legal compliance, and governance. That keeps the mechanics applicable to real organisations while allowing the campaign to explore those ideas through dramatic storytelling



